          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Conceptual orientation",
          "content": "An embryo is a physical system with many local decision-makers rather than a miniature adult unfolding according to a single rigid schedule. At an early stage, one can record cell ancestry, molecular identity, competence, eventual fate and position as separate variables. A complete explanation of an organ therefore needs to establish at least three links: which precursors contribute cells, which interactions specify their identities, and which movements place those cells into a functioning structure. Sometimes these links can be investigated in different model species, but their assumptions must remain visible. A regulatory pathway can be conserved even when the timing of its activation, the relevant tissue and the phenotype of perturbation differ.\nConsider a tissue boundary: a sharp stripe of gene expression appears between two regions with different identities. One hypothesis invokes a concentration threshold in an extracellular ligand. Another invokes direct neighbor-to-neighbor inhibition, and a third invokes selective survival of cells already carrying different identities. A snapshot could support all three explanations. A time series of source position, receptor activation, target-gene transcription, cell division and cell death is more informative. A mosaic perturbation adds a causal intervention. Even then, a damaged cell may modify its neighbors through mechanics, not merely through secreted signals.\nThis unit distinguishes specification from determination and both from the eventual expression of specialized functions. It also develops a basic language for choosing a denominator: fraction of surviving cells, fraction of all labeled descendants, or fraction of an anatomical region. Such choices change interpretation. An intervention that halves a tissue size but leaves half its cells marker-positive may reflect very different biology from an intervention that maintains size while switching half the cells to a new fate. We use deliberately transparent hypothetical numbers to teach these comparisons. The lesson is not to trust a simulated prediction as clinical evidence, but to design observations that would allow incompatible mechanisms to be separated.",
