          "heading": "Conceptual orientation",
          "content": "An egg is unusual because much of the information needed to begin development is already physically arranged within a single cell. This includes cytoplasm, protein stores, localized RNA, membrane properties and organelle distributions. Fertilization often changes that state on a scale of minutes, whereas later rearrangement proceeds across hours or days. Developmental stage is therefore more informative than elapsed time alone. Temperature, species and culture conditions affect the interval between cleavage rounds. Counting cells without recording staging can incorrectly suggest different developmental capacities.\nCleavage provides a useful numerical anchor. Under the ideal synchronized binary-division model, n rounds yield 2^n blastomeres. At roughly conserved total volume, average blastomere volume is initial volume divided by 2^n. Neither equation describes growth, asynchrony or apoptosis; that failure is part of the lesson. A real lineage tree documents which individual cells divided and when, while a volume segmentation test reveals whether the apparent halves truly partitioned the cytoplasm evenly. If a single cell fails to divide, the total count immediately departs from the binary model, but the embryo's downstream development need not fail in direct proportion to that numerical deviation.\nThe subsequent appearance of a blastula or blastocyst is an architectural transformation, not simply one extra cleavage round. The distinction between mammalian blastocyst lineages and the external yolk-associated architectures of other vertebrates matters when designing comparisons. Gastrulation then combines cell rearrangement with fate assignment and axis formation. A colored germ-layer diagram is useful for orientation, but cells in living embryos move through three-dimensional paths and many organs contain contributions from several lineages. The proper measurement might be surface deformation, relative cell velocity, or lineage occupancy of a territory. Each requires a different acquisition strategy.",
