          "secNumber": "3.2",
          "heading": "Conceptual orientation",
          "content": "Embryonic pattern is built through coordinates that cells must infer using limited local information. Sources and sinks shape gradients, signaling networks interpret concentration and duration, and tissue growth changes distances over time. It is tempting to think that a cell reads one molecular ruler and is assigned a permanent address. In real development, multiple axes interact, competence windows open and close, and feedback adjusts input-output relationships. The same molecule may help establish an initial broad polarity and later refine local identities in an organ field.\nThe classic exponential profile C(x)=C0 exp(-x/lambda) illustrates how a localized source can produce different levels of signal along one axis. A cell with a threshold of 0.3 C0 is predicted to switch near x = -lambda ln(0.3). This is a mathematical consequence of a chosen model, not a claim that every embryo has a static exponential morphogen gradient. The gradient length lambda may change with receptor-mediated uptake, extracellular trapping, geometry and growth. Even a perfect concentration measurement would not predict fate unless the intracellular response network and duration of exposure were known.\nSegmentation adds time to position. If a molecular oscillator has period T and the relevant maturation front advances with speed v, a first approximation to segment spacing is vT. But the same final segment length can result from a slower front and longer period or a faster front and shorter period, so imaging only the finished somites cannot uniquely identify the mechanism. Left-right asymmetry offers another caution: an asymmetric reporter can indicate an early step without ensuring correctly arranged organs. Each model in this unit is a hypothesis generator, and students should identify a measurement that could falsify its simplifying assumptions.",
