          "secNumber": "4.2",
          "heading": "Conceptual orientation",
          "content": "Organogenesis requires more than selecting cell types. A nervous system needs folded and connected structures; a limb needs patterned length and digits; a heart requires a tube that loops and connects; and an epithelial organ may need a branching network. Many defects therefore reflect geometry, movement or cell survival despite apparently normal early fate markers. Developmental biology should analyze a final phenotype backwards through the sequence of contributing processes instead of searching for a single defective gene in isolation.\nNeurulation is an especially clear case because its mechanical steps can be observed. Convergent extension, hinge-point formation, apical constriction and tissue attachment alter shape. Different parts of a vertebrate neural tube and different vertebrate taxa do not use exactly the same route. A cell can express a normal neural marker while failing to fold into an appropriate tube. Conversely, an altered tissue boundary can precede a later closure failure, making closure appear mechanically defective when a patterning step was the upstream cause. Stage-resolved imaging and cell-specific interventions are therefore central.\nDuring limb development, a positional signal can affect digit identity while outgrowth signals affect size, yet the two processes interact. A branching epithelium demonstrates a related problem: branch count is easy to report but often biologically inadequate. Useful measurements include branch angles, length distribution, lumen continuity, cell polarity and differentiated function. A heart tube's contraction frequency cannot stand in for chamber identity or normal looping. The integrated laboratory asks students to choose at least two distinct outcome variables for an organ so that a measured change cannot be overinterpreted as a single global improvement or decline.",
