          "secNumber": "5.2",
          "heading": "Conceptual orientation",
          "content": "Embryonic shapes arise from collective cell decisions and material constraints. Adhesion changes who remains in contact; contractility changes forces across those contacts; proliferation adds volume; apoptosis removes cells; and migration or intercalation redistributes neighbors. These contributions can compensate for each other. A final tissue length is thus an outcome of several processes, not a direct measurement of any single one. The practical solution is to acquire movies and quantitative geometry alongside molecular readouts.\nThe path of a moving cell illustrates the measurement problem. Speed is traveled path divided by elapsed time. Net displacement is the straight-line separation of initial and final positions. Directional persistence, often approximated as displacement divided by path length, distinguishes purposeful travel from wandering. Two cells could move the same cumulative distance and arrive at very different final destinations. Three-dimensional movement needs appropriately spaced z-stacks; otherwise a cell can disappear from a focal plane and falsely be interpreted as a dead or stalled cell.\nA mechanical experiment also demands care. When laser ablation cuts an epithelial junction, local recoil is affected by preexisting stress but also by viscosity and architecture. Recoil is therefore an informative proxy under an assumed material model, not a calibrated tensile-force measurement by itself. Similar caution applies to cell sorting: changing cadherin expression can alter adhesion, cell signaling and contractility simultaneously. To infer cause, investigators pair spatial perturbations with complementary measurements and explicit alternatives. Our simulated curves keep assumptions visible and never substitute for direct mechanical calibration.",
